</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Automating Judgement of LLM Summaries with <span id="S3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DeliberationJudge</span></h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While recent work has increasingly adopted pretrained LLMs as automated judges, prior studies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ye et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Thakur et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Krumdick et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2025</a>)</cite> reveal their limitations, including systematic biases and instability. These shortcomings largely stem from their black-box nature, with billions of internal parameters, and from the constraints of current alignment paradigms. To balance efficiency and reliability, we introduce <span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DeliberationJudge</span>, a DeBERTa-based model fine-tuned on a large-scale human judgment dataset tailored to deliberation summarization, which we subsequently employ for automatic evaluation.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Limited Correlations Between Human and LLM Judgments</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To examine consistency between human and LLM judgments, we use the original inputs of <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{test}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>test</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{test}}</annotation></semantics></math>, namely each quadruple <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(q_{i},o^{(a)}_{S},S,S^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>o</mi><mi>S</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(q_{i},o^{(a)}_{S},S,S^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>, and ask LLMs to provide annotations under the same guidelines as humans (see Equation <a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2.2 DeliberationBank ‣ 2 Dataset and Evaluation Setting ‣ Can AI Truly Represent Your Voice in Deliberations? A Comprehensive Study of Large-Scale Opinion Aggregation with LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and Table <a href="#A5.T7" title="Table 7 ‣ E.2 Comparison of different base models ‣ Appendix E Details of Judge Model Training ‣ Can AI Truly Represent Your Voice in Deliberations? A Comprehensive Study of Large-Scale Opinion Aggregation with LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>). The model is not asked to produce new opinions; it only re-annotates the existing inputs. This yields the LLM-labeled set <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathcal{T}}^{\mathrm{LLM}}_{\mathrm{test}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>test</mi><mi>LLM</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathcal{T}}^{\mathrm{LLM}}_{\mathrm{test}}</annotation></semantics></math>, directly comparable to the human-labeled <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{test}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>test</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{test}}</annotation></semantics></math> for correlation analysis.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">After annotation, we compute correlations between LLM and human judgments. As shown in Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3.1 Limited Correlations Between Human and LLM Judgments ‣ 3 Automating Judgement of LLM Summaries with DeliberationJudge ‣ Can AI Truly Represent Your Voice in Deliberations? A Comprehensive Study of Large-Scale Opinion Aggregation with LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, larger models achieve higher agreement with human judges, while very small models (e.g., <span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Qwen3-0.6B</span>, <span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Qwen3-1.7B</span>) perform poorly across all dimensions. In the rating task, alignment is strongest on <span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Representativeness</span> and <span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Policy Approval</span>, with correlations around 0.30–0.35, suggesting these dimensions are easier for LLMs to approximate. In the comparison task, overall correlations are lower, though <span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Informativeness</span> reaches about 0.37. Overall, correlations never exceed 0.4, showing that while LLMs can partially approximate human judgments, off-the-shelf models remain insufficient as automated judges and motivate the need for more reliable, dedicated judge models.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.05154v1/rating_compare_triple_summary.png" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/154;" width="548" height="154" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Heatmap of consistency between human and LLM judges on two judgment tasks. <span id="S3.F3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Left</span>: Rating–Pearson <math id="S3.F3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>; Center: Rating–Spearman <math id="S3.F3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>; Right: Comparison–Spearman <math id="S3.F3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>. Lighter to darker colors represent lower to higher correlations.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span><span id="S3.SS2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DeliberationJudge</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Considering the lack of consistency between LLM as Judge and human annotation, we propose <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DeliberationJudge</span>, a DeBERTa-based judge <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{J}_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{J}_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>, trained with supervised fine-tuning (SFT) on the two types of human annotations from from <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{train}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>train</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{train}}</annotation></semantics></math> introduced in § <a href="#S2.SS2" title="2.2 DeliberationBank ‣ 2 Dataset and Evaluation Setting ‣ Can AI Truly Represent Your Voice in Deliberations? A Comprehensive Study of Large-Scale Opinion Aggregation with LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>. To place both rating and comparison tasks on a unified scale, we normalize the labels for each evaluation dimension. Specifically, five-point Likert scores from the rating task (Summary A alone) are retained on <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[1,5]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[1,5]</annotation></semantics></math>, while five-point relative judgments from the comparison task (Summary A vs. Summary B) are linearly mapped into the extended interval <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[-1,7]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[-1,7]</annotation></semantics></math>. This normalization allows both annotation sources to be trained jointly as graded supervision on the same scale.
Formally, given an input consisting of a deliberation question <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, an annotator opinion <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o^{(a)}_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>o</mi><mi>S</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o^{(a)}_{S}</annotation></semantics></math>, and a candidate summary <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{\mathcal{M},\tilde{\mathcal{O}}_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{\mathcal{M},\tilde{\mathcal{O}}_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> produced by model <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> from opinion subset <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathcal{O}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathcal{O}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, the judge <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{J}_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{J}_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> encodes the concatenated sequence</p>
<table id="S3.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\texttt{[CLS]}\;q_{i}\;\texttt{[SEP]}\;o^{(a)}_{S}\;\texttt{[SEP]}\;S_{\mathcal{M},\tilde{\mathcal{O}}_{i}}\;\texttt{[SEP]}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">[CLS]</mtext><mo lspace="0.280em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">[SEP]</mtext><mo lspace="0.280em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>o</mi><mi>S</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">[SEP]</mtext><mo lspace="0.280em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">[SEP]</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{[CLS]}\;q_{i}\;\texttt{[SEP]}\;o^{(a)}_{S}\;\texttt{[SEP]}\;S_{\mathcal{M},\tilde{\mathcal{O}}_{i}}\;\texttt{[SEP]}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">and outputs a four-dimensional normalized score vector:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{y}}=\mathcal{J}_{\theta}(q_{i},o^{(a)}_{S},S_{\mathcal{M},\tilde{\mathcal{O}}_{i}})=\bigl(\hat{y}^{(\mathrm{rep})},\hat{y}^{(\mathrm{inf})},\hat{y}^{(\mathrm{neu})},\hat{y}^{(\mathrm{pol})}\bigr)\in[0,1]^{4}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>o</mi><mi>S</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>rep</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>inf</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>neu</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>pol</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{y}}=\mathcal{J}_{\theta}(q_{i},o^{(a)}_{S},S_{\mathcal{M},\tilde{\mathcal{O}}_{i}})=\bigl(\hat{y}^{(\mathrm{rep})},\hat{y}^{(\mathrm{inf})},\hat{y}^{(\mathrm{neu})},\hat{y}^{(\mathrm{pol})}\bigr)\in[0,1]^{4}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Here the [CLS] representation from the final encoder layer is passed through a hidden layer and a linear projection to produce the four regression outputs. Human annotations <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}^{\text{raw}}\in[-1,7]^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐲</mi><mtext>raw</mtext></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}^{\text{raw}}\in[-1,7]^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> are linearly normalized to <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}\in[0,1]^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}\in[0,1]^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> for training stability.
The model is trained with the Huber loss averaged across dimensions:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}(\theta)=\frac{1}{|\mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{train}}|}\sum_{(q,o,S)\in\mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{train}}}\frac{1}{4}\sum_{d\in\{\mathrm{rep},\mathrm{inf},\mathrm{neu},\mathrm{pol}\}}\ell_{\delta}\!\left(\hat{y}^{(d)},y^{(d)}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>train</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>o</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>train</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>rep</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>inf</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>neu</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>pol</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}(\theta)=\frac{1}{|\mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{train}}|}\sum_{(q,o,S)\in\mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{train}}}\frac{1}{4}\sum_{d\in\{\mathrm{rep},\mathrm{inf},\mathrm{neu},\mathrm{pol}\}}\ell_{\delta}\!\left(\hat{y}^{(d)},y^{(d)}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.4" class="ltx_p">where</p>
<table id="S3.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{\delta}(\hat{y},y)=\begin{cases}\frac{1}{2}(\hat{y}-y)^{2}&amp;\text{if }|\hat{y}-y|\leq\delta,\\[4.0pt]
\delta\cdot\bigl(|\hat{y}-y|-\tfrac{1}{2}\delta\bigr)&amp;\text{otherwise.}\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext>otherwise.</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{\delta}(\hat{y},y)=\begin{cases}\frac{1}{2}(\hat{y}-y)^{2}&amp;\text{if }|\hat{y}-y|\leq\delta,\\[4.0pt]
\delta\cdot\bigl(|\hat{y}-y|-\tfrac{1}{2}\delta\bigr)&amp;\text{otherwise.}\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.5" class="ltx_p">At inference time, predictions remain in the <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> range and are used directly as summary scores. Detailed training settings are provided in Appendix <a href="#A5.SS1" title="E.1 Multi-Output Regression Model Training ‣ Appendix E Details of Judge Model Training ‣ Can AI Truly Represent Your Voice in Deliberations? A Comprehensive Study of Large-Scale Opinion Aggregation with LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.1</span></a>. We also compared multiple base models and found DeBERTa to perform best, full comparison results are reported in Appendix <a href="#A5.SS2" title="E.2 Comparison of different base models ‣ Appendix E Details of Judge Model Training ‣ Can AI Truly Represent Your Voice in Deliberations? A Comprehensive Study of Large-Scale Opinion Aggregation with LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span><span id="S3.SS3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DeliberationJudge</span> Outperforms SOTA LLMs in Consistency and Efficiency</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate the agreement between human judges and <span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DeliberationJudge</span>, and compare it against Human–LLM judgment correlations as defined in § <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Limited Correlations Between Human and LLM Judgments ‣ 3 Automating Judgement of LLM Summaries with DeliberationJudge ‣ Can AI Truly Represent Your Voice in Deliberations? A Comprehensive Study of Large-Scale Opinion Aggregation with LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>.
Specifically, we provide <span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DeliberationJudge</span> with the same held-out test set <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{test}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>test</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{test}}</annotation></semantics></math> that was used for LLM-based annotation.
While <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{test}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>test</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{test}}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined over quadruples <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(q_{i},o^{(a)}_{S},S,S^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>o</mi><mi>S</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(q_{i},o^{(a)}_{S},S,S^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>,
<span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DeliberationJudge</span> takes as input a pair <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(q_{i},o^{(a)}_{S},S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>o</mi><mi>S</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(q_{i},o^{(a)}_{S},S)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Therefore, each quadruple is converted into two regression instances,
<math id="S3.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(q_{i},o^{(a)}_{S},S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>o</mi><mi>S</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(q_{i},o^{(a)}_{S},S)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(q_{i},o^{(a)}_{S},S^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>o</mi><mi>S</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(q_{i},o^{(a)}_{S},S^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>,
with outputs normalized to <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{test}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>test</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{test}}</annotation></semantics></math> contains <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="900" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>900</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">900</annotation></semantics></math> quadruples, this conversion yields <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1{,}800" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1,800</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1{,}800</annotation></semantics></math> (question, opinion, summary) triples for <span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DeliberationJudge</span>,
forming a new annotated set <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathcal{T}}_{\mathrm{test}}^{\mathrm{Delib}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>test</mi><mi>Delib</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathcal{T}}_{\mathrm{test}}^{\mathrm{Delib}}</annotation></semantics></math> for subsequent correlation analysis.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We then examine the average Pearson correlation of automatic judges (LLMs and <span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DeliberationJudge</span>) with human annotations, as well as their efficiency in producing judgments for each (question, opinion, summary) pair. In Figure <a href="#S3.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 3.3 DeliberationJudge Outperforms SOTA LLMs in Consistency and Efficiency ‣ 3 Automating Judgement of LLM Summaries with DeliberationJudge ‣ Can AI Truly Represent Your Voice in Deliberations? A Comprehensive Study of Large-Scale Opinion Aggregation with LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> (left), we observe that <span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DeliberationJudge</span> achieves the highest scores in both agreement with human preferences and efficiency. Its overall correlation reaches approximately 0.48, outperforming the second-best model, <span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Claude-3.7-Sonnet</span>, which achieves only around 0.20. In terms of efficiency, <span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DeliberationJudge</span> requires only about 0.03 seconds per item, while most LLM judges take more than 8 seconds on average. Although <span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Qwen3-0.6B</span> approaches our method in inference speed, it performs poorly in judgment quality.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.05154v1/delibjudge_perform.svg" id="S3.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/203;" width="548" height="203"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span><span id="S3.F4.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Left</span>: Judging Time vs. Pearson Correlation. <span id="S3.F4.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DeliberationJudge</span> achieves both the lowest time and the highest correlation compared to LLMs.
<span id="S3.F4.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Right</span>: Scaling Stability. Total judging time of <span id="S3.F4.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DeliberationJudge</span> and eight LLMs as the number of comments increases; solid lines show means and shaded areas denote min–max ranges. In the figures, DelibJudge=<span id="S3.F4.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DeliberationJudge</span>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">From another perspective, efficiency becomes even more critical when scaling to large deliberation settings. For example, if we need to evaluate summaries conditioned on thousands of deliberation opinions, the inference time cost quickly becomes prohibitive. As shown in Figure <a href="#S3.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 3.3 DeliberationJudge Outperforms SOTA LLMs in Consistency and Efficiency ‣ 3 Automating Judgement of LLM Summaries with DeliberationJudge ‣ Can AI Truly Represent Your Voice in Deliberations? A Comprehensive Study of Large-Scale Opinion Aggregation with LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> (right), we compute the total time required by <span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DeliberationJudge</span> and the average of eight LLM judges as the number of input opinions increases. Since judgments must be executed once for each (question, opinion, summary) pair, the total cost for LLMs grows rapidly with opinion number. In contrast, the total time for <span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DeliberationJudge</span> remains largely unaffected by input size, owing to its fast per-pair processing speed.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4 </span>Strong Rank Correlation Between <span id="S3.SS4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DeliberationJudge</span> and Human Judgments</h3>

<figure id="S3.T3" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3: </span>Spearman rank correlation (human vs. <span id="S3.T3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DeliberationJudge</span>) across four dimensions.</figcaption>
<table id="S3.T3.4" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S3.T3.4.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T3.4.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_border_tt"></td>
<td id="S3.T3.4.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt">Average</td>
<td id="S3.T3.4.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt">Representativeness</td>
<td id="S3.T3.4.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt">Informativeness</td>
<td id="S3.T3.4.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt">Neutrality</td>
<td id="S3.T3.4.1.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt">Policy Approval</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T3.4.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T3.4.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t">Spearman <math id="S3.T3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S3.T3.4.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t">0.70</td>
<td id="S3.T3.4.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t">0.60</td>
<td id="S3.T3.4.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t">1.00</td>
<td id="S3.T3.4.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t">0.90</td>
<td id="S3.T3.4.2.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t">0.90</td></tr>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate how well <span id="S3.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DeliberationJudge</span> tracks human judgments on the five models used in Stage 2 summarization for building <span id="S3.SS4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DeliberationBank</span>  (§<a href="#S2.SS2" title="2.2 DeliberationBank ‣ 2 Dataset and Evaluation Setting ‣ Can AI Truly Represent Your Voice in Deliberations? A Comprehensive Study of Large-Scale Opinion Aggregation with LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>). Concretely, for each evaluation dimension and overall average score, we compute the Spearman rank correlation across models between human scores and <span id="S3.SS4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DeliberationJudge</span> predictions on <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathcal{T}}_{\mathrm{test}}^{\mathrm{Delib}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>test</mi><mi>Delib</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathcal{T}}_{\mathrm{test}}^{\mathrm{Delib}}</annotation></semantics></math> and the original <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathcal{T}}_{\mathrm{test}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>test</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathcal{T}}_{\mathrm{test}}</annotation></semantics></math>. As shown in Table <a href="#S3.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 3.4 Strong Rank Correlation Between DeliberationJudge and Human Judgments ‣ 3 Automating Judgement of LLM Summaries with DeliberationJudge ‣ Can AI Truly Represent Your Voice in Deliberations? A Comprehensive Study of Large-Scale Opinion Aggregation with LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, <span id="S3.SS4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DeliberationJudge</span> exhibits strong rank alignment overall (<math id="S3.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=0.70" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.70</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=0.70</annotation></semantics></math>), with near-perfect agreement on Informativeness (<math id="S3.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=1.00" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.00</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=1.00</annotation></semantics></math>) and high alignment on Neutrality and Policy Approval (<math id="S3.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=0.90" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.90</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=0.90</annotation></semantics></math> each). Representativeness shows moderate alignment (<math id="S3.SS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=0.60" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.60</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=0.60</annotation></semantics></math>), reflecting minor ordering differences in that dimension.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Benchmarking LLMs for Deliberation Summarization</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate a broad set of language models, covering both proprietary frontier systems and open-weight models across diverse parameter scales (see Table <a href="#A1.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ Appendix A Details of Model Choice ‣ Can AI Truly Represent Your Voice in Deliberations? A Comprehensive Study of Large-Scale Opinion Aggregation with LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> in Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Details of Model Choice ‣ Can AI Truly Represent Your Voice in Deliberations? A Comprehensive Study of Large-Scale Opinion Aggregation with LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>).
For each deliberation question <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}\in\mathcal{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}\in\mathcal{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>, we sample an opinion set <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{i}\subseteq\mathcal{D}_{\text{bench}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>bench</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{i}\subseteq\mathcal{D}_{\text{bench}}</annotation></semantics></math> of size <math id="S4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{O}_{i}|=300" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>300</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{O}_{i}|=300</annotation></semantics></math>. From this pool, opinion subsets <math id="S4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathcal{O}}_{i,n}\subseteq\mathcal{O}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathcal{O}}_{i,n}\subseteq\mathcal{O}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> of size <math id="S4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\tilde{\mathcal{O}}_{i,n}|=n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\tilde{\mathcal{O}}_{i,n}|=n</annotation></semantics></math> are drawn, with
<math id="S4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\in N=\{10,20,30,50,70,90,120,160,200,240,300\}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>20</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>30</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>50</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>70</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>90</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>120</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>160</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>200</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>240</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>300</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\in N=\{10,20,30,50,70,90,120,160,200,240,300\}.</annotation></semantics></math>
For each pair <math id="S4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(q_{i},\tilde{\mathcal{O}}_{i,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(q_{i},\tilde{\mathcal{O}}_{i,n})</annotation></semantics></math>, we perform three independent summary resamplings via</p>
<table id="S4.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="S^{(k)}_{\mathcal{M},\,\tilde{\mathcal{O}}_{i,n}}=\mathcal{M}(q_{i},\,\tilde{\mathcal{O}}_{i,n}),\quad k=1,2,3." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{(k)}_{\mathcal{M},\,\tilde{\mathcal{O}}_{i,n}}=\mathcal{M}(q_{i},\,\tilde{\mathcal{O}}_{i,n}),\quad k=1,2,3.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Each summary <math id="S4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{(k)}_{\mathcal{M},\,\tilde{\mathcal{O}}_{i,n}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{(k)}_{\mathcal{M},\,\tilde{\mathcal{O}}_{i,n}}</annotation></semantics></math> is then evaluated by <span id="S4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DeliberationJudge</span> against every opinion in the corresponding subset.
Formally, for each <math id="S4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{i}^{(j)}\in\tilde{\mathcal{O}}_{i,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{i}^{(j)}\in\tilde{\mathcal{O}}_{i,n}</annotation></semantics></math>, the judge maps</p>
<table id="S4.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{J}_{\theta}\!\bigl(q_{i},\,o_{i}^{(j)},\,S^{(k)}_{\mathcal{M},\,\tilde{\mathcal{O}}_{i,n}}\bigr)=\bigl(\hat{y}^{(\mathrm{rep})},\,\hat{y}^{(\mathrm{inf})},\,\hat{y}^{(\mathrm{neu})},\,\hat{y}^{(\mathrm{pol})}\bigr)\in[0,1]^{4}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>rep</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>inf</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>neu</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>pol</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{J}_{\theta}\!\bigl(q_{i},\,o_{i}^{(j)},\,S^{(k)}_{\mathcal{M},\,\tilde{\mathcal{O}}_{i,n}}\bigr)=\bigl(\hat{y}^{(\mathrm{rep})},\,\hat{y}^{(\mathrm{inf})},\,\hat{y}^{(\mathrm{neu})},\,\hat{y}^{(\mathrm{pol})}\bigr)\in[0,1]^{4}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Finally, results from the three resampled summaries are aggregated to compute 95% confidence interval (CI) for each evaluation metric. Finally, results from the three resampled summaries are aggregated to compute
95% confidence intervals (CI) for each evaluation metric.To report a global-wise average score on four dimensions for model <math id="S4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, we first take the mean across the four
evaluation dimensions for each summary:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{(k)}_{i,n,j}=\tfrac{1}{4}\Bigl(\hat{y}^{(\mathrm{rep})}+\hat{y}^{(\mathrm{inf})}+\hat{y}^{(\mathrm{neu})}+\hat{y}^{(\mathrm{pol})}\Bigr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>rep</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>inf</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>neu</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>pol</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{(k)}_{i,n,j}=\tfrac{1}{4}\Bigl(\hat{y}^{(\mathrm{rep})}+\hat{y}^{(\mathrm{inf})}+\hat{y}^{(\mathrm{neu})}+\hat{y}^{(\mathrm{pol})}\Bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p1.4" class="ltx_p">The model’s global average score (GAS) is then defined as</p>
<table id="S4.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="GAS(\mathcal{M})=\frac{1}{|\mathcal{Q}|}\sum_{i=1}^{|\mathcal{Q}|}\frac{1}{|N|}\sum_{n\in N}\frac{1}{n}\sum_{j=1}^{n}\frac{1}{3}\sum_{k=1}^{3}y^{(k)}_{i,n,j}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></munderover><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>3</mn></munderover><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">GAS(\mathcal{M})=\frac{1}{|\mathcal{Q}|}\sum_{i=1}^{|\mathcal{Q}|}\frac{1}{|N|}\sum_{n\in N}\frac{1}{n}\sum_{j=1}^{n}\frac{1}{3}\sum_{k=1}^{3}y^{(k)}_{i,n,j},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p1.5" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{Q}|=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{Q}|=10</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the number of questions,
<math id="S4.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=\{10,20,\ldots,300\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>20</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>300</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=\{10,20,\ldots,300\}</annotation></semantics></math> is the set of subset sizes, and <math id="S4.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j=1,\ldots,n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j=1,\ldots,n</annotation></semantics></math> indexes the opinions within each subset <math id="S4.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathcal{O}}_{i,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathcal{O}}_{i,n}</annotation></semantics></math>. Beyond this global score, we later conduct fine-grained analyses by computing subset-wise and question-wise average scores.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T4" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 4: </span>Model’s global average score (GAS), expressed as <math id="S4.T4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="GAS\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo>±</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">GAS\pm</annotation></semantics></math> 95% CI half-width, computed across four evaluation dimensions (Representativeness, Informativeness, Neutrality, and Policy Approval) over all data samples. Models are arranged from left to right and top to bottom in descending order of mean performance.</figcaption>
